<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    v0.41.0: warm cream paper.

    The previous background was a dusk-sky gradient (navy at the
    top, slate at the bottom) with a low ridge. It was coherent
    with the home shell's late-night rendering, but at 48dp on the
    app grid it read as "a dark app" — exactly the first-impression
    the BDP-safe design language is trying to avoid. A user
    installing MindAnchor in distress is already in the dark; the
    icon should not be.

    The new background is a single warm cream (#FAF6EE), the same
    paper the v0.38.0 breathing surface draws. There is no gradient,
    no ridge, no chrome — the foreground mark is the entire picture.
    The icon now reads as "a quiet place" the moment the eye lands
    on the app grid, which is the design intent.

    Everything here bleeds past the safe zone on purpose: the
    background layer is what gets masked and parallaxed by the
    launcher, so it must have no edges of its own.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!-- Warm cream paper. One path, no gradient. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FAF6EE"
        android:pathData="M0,0h108v108h-108z" />
</vector>
